    "result_text": "All tests pass. Here's a summary of the change.\n\n## What changed\n\nThe `customerCode` format went from `<LAST3>-<NNNN>` (global sequence) to `<CITY3>-<LAST3>-<NNNN>` with a **per-city** sequence.\n\n**`CustomerCodeGenerator`** \u2014 `generate` now takes `(city, lastName, sequence)` and emits `<CITY3>-<LAST3>-<NNNN>`. I factored the \"upper-cased, trimmed first three letters\" logic into a private `prefix(...)` helper reused for both city and last name, so the rule lives in one place.\n\n**`OwnerRepository`** \u2014 added `countByCityIgnoreCase(String city)` so the service can ask \"how many owners already in this city?\" without loading them all. Implemented in all three backends to keep them consistent:\n- `SpringDataOwnerRepository` (the active `spring-data-jpa` profile) \u2014 derived query method.\n- `JpaOwnerRepositoryImpl` \u2014 `SELECT COUNT ... WHERE LOWER(city) = LOWER(:city)`.\n- `JdbcOwnerRepositoryImpl` \u2014 matching SQL count.\n\n**`ClinicServiceImpl.saveOwner`** \u2014 computes the per-city sequence as `countByCityIgnoreCase(city) + 1` and passes city + lastName + sequence to the generator.\n\n**Values derived from `customerCode`** \u2014 the membership number in `OwnerMapper.formatMembershipNumber` builds directly off `getCustomerCode()`, so it automatically inherits the new format (e.g. `LON-SMI-0007-M26`); I only updated its javadoc example to match.\n\nThe acceptance test `coreCustomerCodeHasCityPrefix` (asserting `[A-Z]{3}-[A-Z]{3}-\\d{4}`) passes, and the full suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`).",
